1
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:07,880
A lot of us are overwhelmed, pissed, and scared.

2
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:11,000
I don't want to wait till the next election.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,780
I don't want to just write my congressperson and hope that they'll do the right thing.

4
00:00:16,340 --> 00:00:21,010
I don't want to just post things to the vacuum of social media.

5
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:26,390
I don't want to just make statements about things.

6
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:28,250
I want to change how things are.

7
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:34,440
There are a zillion things we can do, and people are coming up with new ones all the time.

8
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:41,500
This video is going to focus on mutual aid projects, what they are, and why we should be developing them right now.

9
00:00:44,030 --> 00:00:51,130
Mutual aid projects are a form of political participation in which people take responsibility for caring for one another and changing political conditions.

10
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:58,670
Not just through symbolic acts or putting pressure on their representatives and government, but by actually building new social relations that are more survivable.

11
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:03,690
During recent flooding and storms, we saw mutual aid in action as people helped each other survive.

12
00:01:03,690 --> 00:01:07,710
Mutual aid isn't just for those big-weather disasters.

13
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:12,270
It is also for the daily, routine, life-threatening disasters of capitalism and white supremacy.

14
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:24,560
People who are pissed about police in prisons are doing mutual aid work like creating prison letter-writing projects,

15
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:27,600
where people get connected to pen pals in prison to build relationships,

16
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:31,340
help prepare for release, help prisoners have advocates on the outside,

17
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:37,540
and help build a movement against policing in prisons that is informed and led by the people who know the very most about how the system actually works.

18
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:40,000
Some people are creating jail support programs,

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,880
where people get together to make a schedule and agree to be outside their local jail on that schedule

20
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:46,600
and greet anyone getting out and help them get what they need.

21
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:51,660
Maybe a ride, clothes, a phone to call contacts, information about services or benefits.

22
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,800
Some people are organizing community bail funds,

23
00:01:56,140 --> 00:02:00,300
revolving funds that can pay people's bail so that they are not locked up while they try to prepare for their defense.

24
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:05,220
Money bail systems are one of the ways that poor people and people of color are given the worst chances in the criminal system.

25
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:11,320
Court support projects are where people coordinate to accompany someone facing charges to their court dates,

26
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:15,500
ideally by packing the court with supporters each time so that no one has to go alone,

27
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,100
and sometimes to influence lawyers, judges, and jury by showing their support for the criminalized person.

28
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:25,400
Some people are coordinating ride systems to help families visit prisoners who are being held in facilities far from home.

29
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:30,780
In Oakland, the Oakland Power Projects are about strengthening people's skills to respond to community emergencies

30
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:32,620
in ways that minimize police contact.

31
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:36,880
When you call 911 for a health emergency, the cops come too, and that often leads to violence.

32
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:42,460
The Oakland Power Projects is about training the community to respond to health emergencies,

33
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:45,780
including mental health crises, chronic health problems, and acute health emergencies,

34
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:47,180
so that people don't have to call 911.

35
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:52,760
People who are scared about the emboldenment of ICE and Border Patrol and increasing deportations

36
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:54,980
are doing things like forming rapid response networks,

37
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:58,120
where people warn each other about immigration raids and help each other hide,

38
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:01,180
and helping immigrants do safety planning in case they get detained

39
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:03,340
so that someone is ready to take care of their kids and elders.

40
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:07,000
Some rapid response projects are even working on training people to show up

41
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,320
and physically stop ICE from taking someone away.

42
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:13,440
Imagine if we built that kind of power to stop arrests through rapid mobilization

43
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:15,080
of a lot of people to outnumber cops.

44
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:20,000
No More Deaths, an organization in Arizona, works to save the lives of people crossing the border

45
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,880
by putting food, water, and supplies in the harsh desert areas

46
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:25,140
where people who are crossing often die from the conditions.

47
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,040
There are so many mutual aid project possibilities

48
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:32,320
because there are so many intense ways people aren't having their needs met

49
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:33,760
in the brutal systems we live under.

50
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:36,580
Like food projects like Food Not Bombs,

51
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:40,480
projects where people organize temporary housing for people coming out of prison or foster care

52
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:41,880
by opening their homes to each other,

53
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,900
childcare collectives where people watch each other's kids

54
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:47,060
so they can go to political meetings, court, or jobs,

55
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:50,300
projects where people accompany vulnerable people like trans people

56
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:52,120
or people with disabilities to medical appointments

57
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:53,780
or public benefits offices and hearings,

58
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:57,060
projects where people make sure neighbors being pushed out by gentrification

59
00:03:57,060 --> 00:03:59,260
have good access to information about their housing rights

60
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:00,720
and accompany each other to housing court,

61
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:03,240
help people read documents and defend themselves from eviction,

62
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:06,520
projects where people protest landlords who are refusing to make repairs

63
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:07,820
or give back security deposits

64
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:10,540
by directly protesting at those landlords' houses and businesses.

65
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:19,940
The messages of this work are

66
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:21,960
the government is fixed, we can't rely on it.

67
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:24,700
You are not alone.

68
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:29,580
The system is the problem, not the person being targeted by it.

69
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:32,320
And we're going to take matters into our own hands

70
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:33,960
and help each other survive right now

71
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,260
rather than expecting help from the same systems

72
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:37,800
that have a clear history of causing harm.

73
00:04:40,410 --> 00:04:42,730
Mutual aid projects don't just help with the current disasters,

74
00:04:43,150 --> 00:04:44,930
they help us prepare for the ongoing disasters

75
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:47,810
that are emerging because of climate chaos and crumbling infrastructure.

76
00:04:47,810 --> 00:04:53,810
When we build cooperative projects,

77
00:04:54,250 --> 00:04:55,550
practice making decisions together,

78
00:04:56,070 --> 00:04:56,730
share things,

79
00:04:57,270 --> 00:04:58,410
meet more people in our communities

80
00:04:58,410 --> 00:05:00,350
and learn about each other's skills and needs

81
00:05:00,350 --> 00:05:02,830
and learn how current systems work

82
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:03,910
and how they are not working,

83
00:05:04,270 --> 00:05:05,910
we're better prepared for the next storm,

84
00:05:06,210 --> 00:05:07,090
the next blackout,

85
00:05:07,190 --> 00:05:08,230
and the next budget cuts.

86
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:14,540
Something really important about all this

87
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:16,880
is that mutual aid is not charity.

88
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:21,180
Charity is where rich people and institutions

89
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:22,640
give tiny crumbs to poor people

90
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:23,720
to make themselves look better.

91
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:28,440
Usually there are a lot of strings attached to what they give,

92
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:30,680
like giving only to mothers or only to children,

93
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:32,880
only to sober people or only to people of faith.

94
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:35,320
Charity rides on the idea that rich people

95
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:36,940
or social workers should decide

96
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:38,060
who is the deserving poor

97
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:39,660
and who is the undeserving poor,

98
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:41,480
and that rich people can put conditions

99
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:43,740
on the housing or food or cash they give poor people.

100
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:46,840
Charity blames poor people for poverty.

101
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:49,940
Mutual aid blames the system for making people poor

102
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:52,120
and says everyone deserves everything they need.

103
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:56,800
Charity affirms the existing distribution

104
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,160
of wealth and life chances.

105
00:05:58,420 --> 00:05:59,580
Mutual aid challenges it.

106
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:03,180
Charity is top-down.

107
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:04,400
Mutual aid is horizontal.

108
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:11,480
Charity is about control, hierarchy, and isolation.

109
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,180
Mutual aid is about solidarity,

110
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:16,040
liberation, and participation.

111
00:06:27,230 --> 00:06:28,690
People are scared and angry right now

112
00:06:28,690 --> 00:06:30,250
and trying to find ways to fight back

113
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:31,070
and support each other.

114
00:06:31,610 --> 00:06:32,810
Building mutual aid projects

115
00:06:32,810 --> 00:06:34,170
is a way to plug people in,

116
00:06:34,610 --> 00:06:36,530
build shared understandings of current conditions,

117
00:06:37,150 --> 00:06:39,190
offer meaningful support to vulnerable people,

118
00:06:39,190 --> 00:06:40,670
and prepare for the coming disasters.

119
00:06:42,170 --> 00:06:44,070
Mutual aid work is not easy.

120
00:06:44,530 --> 00:06:46,030
It means forming lasting commitments

121
00:06:46,030 --> 00:06:47,150
to doing hard work,

122
00:06:47,550 --> 00:06:48,490
collaborating with people

123
00:06:48,490 --> 00:06:49,630
even when we have conflict,

124
00:06:50,010 --> 00:06:51,570
and facing the heart-wrenching realities

125
00:06:51,570 --> 00:06:52,750
of the systems we live under.

126
00:06:53,390 --> 00:06:55,710
It is also deeply satisfying work

127
00:06:55,710 --> 00:06:56,790
that transforms us

128
00:06:56,790 --> 00:06:59,010
from being exasperated, passive observers

129
00:06:59,010 --> 00:07:00,390
of the shitstorm we're living in

130
00:07:00,390 --> 00:07:02,990
to inspired builders of the new world

131
00:07:02,990 --> 00:07:04,270
we desperately crave in need.

132
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:07,660
Stop believing in authority

133
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:09,120
and start believing in each other.

134
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:15,300
We're all we've got,

135
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:16,760
we're all we need.

136
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:17,320
We're all we need.

